// AwsImportNetwork names the VPC to import. The controller (not the API
// server) looks the rest up once, read-only, as the tenant role of the
// account (the role its ProviderConfig assumes too):
//
//	ec2:DescribeVpcs              the VPC
//	ec2:DescribeSubnets           subnet-id=<subnet_ids> (each must be in the VPC)
//	ec2:DescribeRouteTables       vpc-id (routes, associations, main table)
//	ec2:DescribeInternetGateways  attachment.vpc-id
//	ec2:DescribeNatGateways       vpc-id (+ their EIP allocations)
//	ec2:DescribeSecurityGroups    vpc-id, group-name=default
//	ec2:DescribeNetworkAcls       vpc-id (the default and the custom ones)
//	ec2:DescribeFlowLogs          the region's (those on the VPC are imported)
//	logs:DescribeLogGroups, logs:ListTagsForResource, iam:GetRole
//	                              for flow log destinations
//
// and creates an Observe-only Crossplane managed resource for each result
// it imports.
// Modelled (spec.layout): VPC, subnets, their route tables + associations,
// the 0.0.0.0/0 routes, internet gateway, NAT gateways + EIPs. A subnet is
// PUBLIC when its route table routes to the internet gateway (0.0.0.0/0 or
// specific CIDRs only), PRIVATE when its default route is a NAT gateway.
// CARRIED_OVER (import.proto):
//   - flow logs on the VPC that deliver to CloudWatch Logs (FlowLog), with
//     their log group (cloudwatchlogs Group) and delivery role (iam Role);
//     a log group or role that flow logs of other VPCs use too is owned
//     without Delete
//   - the default network ACL (DefaultNetworkACL, rules inline: that kind
//     owns them); it is never deleted
//   - custom network ACLs associated with an imported subnet (NetworkACL,
//     subnets in subnetIds) with their rules (NetworkACLRule)
//   - routes of specific CIDRs to the internet gateway in imported route
//     tables (Route)
//
// NOT_IMPORTED: the VPC's main route table (subnets without a route table
// of their own keep using it), other routes (peering, Transit Gateway,
// VPN, ...), the default security group, flow logs to S3 or Firehose or
// whose log group or role cannot be read, network ACLs of subnets that are
// not imported, unused NAT gateways, secondary VPC CIDRs. VPC endpoints
// and flow logs on subnets are not looked at.
// From then on status comes from their status.atProvider; the lookup does
// not run again.
